{"qid":"Q484195","names":["Timurid Empire"],"title":"Timurid Empire","endonym":{"native":"تیموریان","lang":"fa"},"capsule":"The Timurid Empire was founded by Timur (Tamerlane), a Turkic-Mongol warlord from Kesh in Transoxiana, who between 1370 and his death in 1405 conquered an arc from Anatolia and Syria through Persia, Central Asia, and northern India, sacking Delhi in 1398 and defeating the Ottoman sultan Bayezid I at Ankara in 1402. After Timur's death the empire fragmented among his sons and grandsons; Shahrukh (r. 1409–1447) consolidated rule from Herat, presiding over a brilliant Persianate court culture, while Ulugh Beg built an observatory at Samarkand and produced star catalogues of lasting scientific value. The last major Timurid sultans were progressively displaced by the Uzbek Shaybanids, who captured Samarkand in 1500 and Herat in 1507, ending the empire's Central Asian core; the line survived only in Kabul under Babur, who went on to found the Mughal dynasty in India.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Timurid core territories in Khorasan, Transoxiana, and Iran are reconstructed from administrative and chronicle sources with reasonable confidence; the empire's more distant tributaries and frontier zones — notably in Anatolia after Ankara and in the eastern steppes — are conjecturally mapped.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Timurid dynasty","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Timurid-dynasty"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Timurid Empire","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timurid_Empire"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Timurid Empire","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name